<main id="main-content" class="feature-page feature-detail-page">
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">arc alternative</span></nav>
  <section class="feature-hero feature-hero--detail feature-hero--text" aria-labelledby="arc-title">
    <h1 id="arc-title">Looking for an Arc alternative? Here is what Blanc does.</h1>
    <p>In May 2025, The Browser Company wrote that it had stopped actively building new features for Arc, while continuing Chromium upgrades and security fixes. If you are deciding what to use next, this page sets out what Blanc offers today, and what it does not.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="https://browsercompany.substack.com/p/letter-to-arc-members-2025">Read The Browser Company’s letter to Arc members <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
  </section>

  <section class="feature-copy-grid feature-copy-grid--top" aria-labelledby="arc-carry-title">
    <div><h2 id="arc-carry-title">A calm browser built around one small island.</h2></div>
    <div class="feature-copy-list">
      <article><h3>Tabs down the side, if you want them.</h3><p>Switch on <a href="/features/vertical-tabs">optional vertical tabs</a> for a persistent, resizable tab list. The island stays your address bar and command surface.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Sets of tabs for different work.</h3><p><a href="/features/tab-groups">Named tab groups</a> keep related tabs together and fold away when you want a quieter view. <a href="/features/workspaces">Blanc Spaces</a>, free for everyone, save a window’s tabs and groups so you can switch between sets in place.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Two pages side by side.</h3><p><a href="/features/glance">Glance</a> keeps a reference tab beside the page you are using. Resize the split, swap the pages or close the reference.</p></article>
      <article><h3>One shortcut for everything.</h3><p>Press ⌘L on a Mac or Ctrl+L on Windows and Linux to <a href="/features/command-palette">search the web, switch tabs, find history and favorites, or run slash commands</a>.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Separate profiles.</h3><p><a href="/features/profiles">Local profiles</a> separate cookies, site data, Favorites, history and permissions, with no account required.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Ads blocked from the first launch.</h3><p><a href="/features/ad-blocking">Blanc Blocker</a> is on by default. On supported builds you can choose full uBlock Origin for regular tabs instead.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature-copy-grid" aria-labelledby="arc-move-title">
    <div><h2 id="arc-move-title">Bring your Arc Spaces with you.</h2></div>
    <div class="feature-copy-list">
      <article><h3>Your Spaces, pinned tabs and folders.</h3><p>Open Bring Your Tabs (type <code>/bring-tabs</code> in the ⌘L or Ctrl+L panel), choose Arc and pick the Spaces you want. Each one comes across with its pinned tabs, its Today tabs, and its folders as tab groups. Each Space becomes a space in <a href="/features/workspaces">Blanc Spaces</a>. Tabs load only when you open them.</p></article>
      <article><h3>What stays in Arc.</h3><p>Blanc reads Arc’s sidebar file on your computer and never changes it. Arc’s top-row Favorites can go into a Favorites folder named Arc. Sign-ins, passwords, history, archived tabs, Notes and Easels stay in Arc. Arc never came to Linux, so there you choose a <code>StorableSidebar.json</code> copied from a Mac or PC.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Bookmarks from other browsers.</h3><p>Blanc can import bookmarks into Favorites from Chromium-based browsers installed on your computer, such as Chrome, Edge and Brave.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Free, and open source.</h3><p>Blanc is free for macOS, Windows and Linux. Blanc’s own code is MIT-licensed and public on GitHub; its name, logos and third-party components keep their own terms.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <aside class="truth-note" aria-labelledby="arc-note-title"><h2 id="arc-note-title">Blanc is not a copy of Arc.</h2><p>Blanc does not install extensions from the Chrome Web Store, bring over your Arc sign-ins, passwords or history, or include an AI assistant. If you rely on any of those, Blanc may not be the right fit today.</p></aside>

  <section class="feature-close" aria-labelledby="arc-close-title"><h2 id="arc-close-title">See if one small island suits you.</h2><a class="cta" href="/download" data-track="feature_cta_click" data-feature="arc-alternative" data-cta-position="feature-close">download blanc</a></section>
</main>
